      [9] Samir Sapra, Michael Theobald, Edmund M. Clarke, ``SAT-Based
      Algorithms for Logic Minimization'', ICCD, 2003,pp. 510-515
    </tm-par>
    
    <tm-par>
      <example>
        <tm-par>
          Cyclic Core Example
        </tm-par>
        
        <tm-par>
          
        </tm-par>
        
        <tm-par xml:space="preserve">If Figure 1 represents the geometric realization of a cyclic core, then any of the primes 1-6 are collapsible. Table 1 represents the face incidence relation, from which the graded table can be generated.  Each 1 in the table refers to the fact that the row cell intersects the column prime(which in this case also corresponds to a row cell) in one lower dimensional face.</tm-par>
        
        <tm-par>
          
        </tm-par>
        
        <tm-par xml:space="preserve">If  cell c<rsup>2</rsup><rsub>1</rsub> is chosen as the collapsible cell, then it is removed, and the 1 dimesional cell on the outside, between cells 6 and 2 replaces it.  Note that there are now three additional free faces, and the complex is still a cyclic core.  Proceeding recursively, the whole one skeleton is outlined, until finally the recursion is broken, and the process quickly pops back up to the beginning, at each stage choosing a sequence of primes wich minimally cover S. </tm-par>
      </example>
    </tm-par>
    
    <tm-par>
      
